A||||3||ferg||need help with parking brake light||||1||
Z||000000||ferg||06-15-2001||09:30 AM||||I drove my car last night for the first time in over a year. When I was putting it away for the night, I applied the brake to shift out of park, the brake pedal went to the floor and then the parking brake light came on. Also I had a lot of smoke coming from the right front tire, I haven't looked at the cause yet, but I'm thinking that I either have a leaking brake line or the caliper is stuck and I'm burning up my brake pads.<P>I am positive that it's not the actual parking brake because I haven't used it. <P>I read in an old post that the parking brake light is shared with both the parking brake and the master cylinder. <P>Is the parking brake light an indication that my master cylinder is going bad? Is the master cylinder still available at the parts store?<P>Could something else cause the parking brake light to come on? Like a possible leaky brake line, or a stuck caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tweek||06-15-2001||09:45 AM||||From my experience, the light comes on when there is a lack of pressure in the system. Such as when I blew out a rusty brake line by slammin on the brakes, and also later that day after repairs it came on when the system was not bled properly, and there was still air in there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||06-15-2001||10:17 AM||||The warning light will illuminate when part of the system developes a leak.  There is a shuttle valve tied to the light switch which stays in the centered position as long as the pressure in the brake system is equal on both circuits.  If the pressure drops in one of the circuits it causes the shuttle valve to move towards the low pressure side and turn the warning light on.<P>Once you repair the leak and bleed the system the valve will re-center itself and turn the light off.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||ferg||06-15-2001||12:18 PM||||Thanks for the help. I'll have to find out what the problem is when I get home.||||reg||1
